Kubernetes and OpenShift Command-Line Interfaces (CLI)
Objectives
Access an OpenShift cluster by using the Kubernetes and OpenShift command-line interfaces.

1. What is CLI in Kubernetes and OpenShift?
A Command Line Interface (CLI) is a tool that allows users to interact with a cluster by typing commands in the terminal.
In Kubernetes and OpenShift, you can manage the cluster in two ways:
1.	Web Console (GUI) – browser interface
2.	CLI (Command Line Interface) – terminal commands
Two main CLIs are used:
CLI	Used For
kubectl	Kubernetes clusters
oc	OpenShift clusters
________________________________________
2. kubectl vs oc
kubectl
•	Native command-line tool for Kubernetes
•	Used to manage Kubernetes resources like:
o	Pods
o	Deployments
o	Services
o	ConfigMaps
Example:
kubectl get pods
________________________________________
oc (OpenShift CLI)
The oc command is a superset of kubectl.
Meaning:
oc = kubectl + OpenShift features
So oc supports:
•	All kubectl commands
•	Plus OpenShift specific commands
Example OpenShift resources:
•	Projects
•	Routes
•	ImageStreams
Example command:
oc new-project myapp
________________________________________
Why use oc CLI?
OpenShift CLI is useful when:
•	Working with source code
•	Writing automation scripts
•	Low bandwidth environments
•	Web console unavailable
•	Managing OpenShift resources
________________________________________
3. Installing kubectl CLI
Step 1 — Download kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
curl	download file
-L	follow redirect
-O	save with original name
This downloads the kubectl binary.
________________________________________
Step 2 — Download checksum
Checksum verifies that the file is not corrupted.
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/.../kubectl.sha256"
________________________________________
Step 3 — Verify file integrity
echo "$(cat kubectl.sha256) kubectl" | sha256sum --check
Output:
kubectl: OK
Meaning the file is valid.
If corrupted:
kubectl: FAILED
________________________________________
Step 4 — Install kubectl
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
Explanation:
Option	Meaning
-o root	owner
-g root	group
-m 0755	permission
________________________________________
Step 5 — Verify installation
kubectl version --client
Output example:
Client Version: v1.31.1
________________________________________
4. Viewing kubectl Commands
To see all commands:
kubectl --help
Example output commands:
Command	Purpose
create	create resource
expose	expose pod as service
run	run container
set	modify object settings
________________________________________
Command specific help
kubectl create --help
Shows:
•	syntax
•	examples
•	options
•	subcommands
Example:
kubectl create -f pod.yaml
Creates a resource using a YAML file.
________________________________________
5. kubectl explain Command
This command shows documentation of resources.
Example:
kubectl explain pod
Output shows:
•	description
•	fields
•	API version
Example fields:
apiVersion
metadata
spec
status
________________________________________
Example deeper explanation:
kubectl explain pods.spec.containers.resources
Explains:
•	CPU
•	memory
•	limits
•	requests
________________________________________
6. OpenShift CLI (oc)
Main tool for managing OpenShift clusters.
Basic syntax:
oc command
Examples:
oc login
oc get pods
oc new-project
________________________________________
7. Downloading oc CLI
You can download it from the OpenShift Web Console.
Path:
Help (?) → Command Line Tools
Available for:
•	Windows
•	Linux
•	Mac
•	ARM systems
________________________________________
8. Authentication in OpenShift
Before using CLI, you must login to the cluster.
Authentication verifies who you are.
Authorization checks what you can do.
________________________________________
Types of Users in OpenShift
1. Regular Users
Normal users like:
•	developers
•	admins
Represented by User objects.
________________________________________
2. System Users
Used by internal infrastructure.
Examples:
•	cluster admin
•	system services
________________________________________
3. Service Accounts
Used by applications to access API.
Example:
pods calling API
Created automatically in projects.
________________________________________
9. Login to OpenShift Cluster
Method 1 — Username and Password
oc login -u admin https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Then it asks for password.
Example:
Username: admin
Password: redhatocp
________________________________________
Simplified command
oc login -u admin -p redhatocp https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
⚠ Not secure for production.
________________________________________
Method 2 — Token Authentication (Recommended)
Used in:
•	production
•	CI/CD pipelines
•	automation scripts
Command:
oc login --token=TOKEN --server=cluster-url
________________________________________
Method 3 — Web Login
oc login --web api-url
This opens a browser authentication page.
________________________________________
10. Projects in OpenShift
A Project is similar to a Kubernetes Namespace.
It provides:
•	isolation
•	multi-tenancy
Create project:
oc new-project myapp
________________________________________
11. Important oc Commands
1. oc cluster-info
Shows cluster information.
oc cluster-info
Example output:
Kubernetes control plane is running at https://api...
Useful for debugging.
________________________________________
2. oc api-versions
Shows API versions supported by the cluster.
oc api-versions
Example:
apps/v1
admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
________________________________________
3. oc get clusteroperator
Shows OpenShift cluster operators.
oc get clusteroperator
Example:
NAME             VERSION   AVAILABLE
authentication   4.18      True
Operators manage OpenShift components automatically.
________________________________________
12. oc get Command
Used to retrieve resource information.
Example:
oc get pods
Output:
NAME                READY   STATUS
quotes-api          1/1     Running
________________________________________
Export resource YAML
oc get pod mypod -o yaml
Used for:
•	backup
•	editing configuration
________________________________________
13. oc get all
Shows all important resources in project.
Command:
oc get all
Output includes:
•	pods
•	services
•	deployments
•	replica sets
•	image streams
________________________________________
14. oc describe Command
Shows detailed information about resources.
Example:
oc describe pod mypod
Displays:
•	node location
•	IP
•	events
•	status
•	labels
•	container info
Useful for troubleshooting.
________________________________________
15. oc explain Command
Shows documentation for resource fields.
Example:
oc explain pods
Example deeper:
oc explain pods.spec.containers
________________________________________
16. oc create Command
Creates resources from YAML or JSON file.
Example:
oc create -f pod.yaml
Output:
pod/quotes-pod created
________________________________________
17. oc status
Shows high-level overview of project.
Command:
oc status
Shows:
•	services
•	deployments
•	builds
•	errors
________________________________________
18. oc delete Command
Deletes resources.
Example:
oc delete pod quotes-ui
Output:
pod/quotes-ui deleted
Important concept:
If a pod is managed by Deployment, it will automatically recreate.
________________________________________
19. Using Namespace / Project
To run command in specific namespace:
oc get pods -n openshift-apiserver
or
--namespace
Example:
oc get pods --namespace=myproject
________________________________________
